## 6. Competitive Landscape

**What already exists on this topic:**

- McKinsey "State of AI" annual reports dominate this space — high authority, broad, focused on enterprise ROI and adoption rates. They cover data readiness but treat it as one of many factors, not a prerequisite.
- Harvard Business Review publishes "how incumbents can adopt AI" content regularly — usually framed around organizational change management. Strong on the people side, weak on data architecture specifics.
- Gartner AI maturity models are well-known but abstracted into analyst frameworks — not practitioner-authored, not grounded in real client scenarios.
- LinkedIn thought leadership ("AI won't replace you, someone using AI will") floods the channel but rarely goes deeper than surface-level encouragement.
- Developer-focused outlets (InfoQ, The New Stack) cover AI code security risks well but don't bridge to the business model conversation.

**What this article has that the competitive field doesn't:**

- A practitioner (not an analyst) explaining the data architecture prerequisite as a hard condition, not a recommendation
- The inside-the-fence data access problem (surfacing salary data to the wrong internal user) — almost entirely absent from business-level AI content
- The free-vs-paid AI tool distinction as a corporate data risk — present in IT security content but not mainstream business strategy content
- Real client examples from a consulting firm doing the work (not survey-derived)
- The mandate-vs-capability adoption frame — practically absent from competitor content

**Differentiation angle:** This article should own the "data estate as the real AI prerequisite" argument. Most content treats data as a dependency; this piece argues it is the foundation without which AI adoption is security theater.

**Featured snippet / AI overview opportunities:**
- "What does a company need before implementing AI?" — H2 targeting this question directly
- "How do employees accidentally leak company data to AI tools?" — H2 or H3 with the 232-incidents stat

**Primary search intent:** Informational — someone searching "how to adopt AI in my business" or "AI business model risks" who is at the awareness-to-consideration stage of an AI strategy decision.

